On 6/2/13 4:45 PM, Yilong wrote:
> Hi All:
>
> I am working on a project which try to fit a time varying coefficient logistic model. Is there any way to implement this
> model into stan ?

All you need to be able to do in Stan is write
down the log probability function.

Or any reference about time varying coefficient Bayesian modeling will be appreciate. Thanks in
> advance.  The model I want to fit is
>
> y_i(t) | p_i(t) ~ Bernoulli( p_i(t) )
> p_i(t) = beta(t) * x
> beta(t) ~ Normal(0, 1/100)
>
> I am thinking there should be a smooth penalize for beta(t) but I did not know how to add it in my model.

Following your subject, I'd be inclined to put beta on the
logistic scale and model it as usual.  In Stan, the model
would be:

   model {
     // priors
     sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
     beta[1] ~ normal(0,1);
     for (t in 2:T)
       beta[t] ~ normal(beta[t-1],sigma);

     // likelihood
     for (n in 1:N)
       for (t in 1:T)
         y[n,t] ~ bernoulli(inv_logit(beta[t] * x[n,t]));
   }

The likelihood is more efficiently and arithmetically stably
expressed using vectorization and the bernoulli_logit distribution
as:

    for (n in 1:N)
      y[n] ~ bernoulli_logit(beta * x[n]);

The logit parameterization of the Bernoulli is just defined
by

     bernoull_logit(...) =def= bernoulli(inv_logit(...))

- Bob

Thanks Ben and Bob for your kindly reply. I actually simplified my question above. Due to the structure of my model, I both need column and row vectors to define a muti_normal and Dirichlet distribution. (That's the reason why I did not use vector[M] tmp[N] )   Below is my code that works in stan. But, I feel uncomfortable about the "transformed parameters" part. Even through the speed to draw samples from this model is quick, it waste memory to store the transform of matrix alpha.

I feel col(tmp, i) should works in my situation. However, while I have a three dimension array like

real tmp[M,N,P];

how could I get row, column and ranks vector and some sub-matrix like tmp[1, , ] in a same framework ?

|

data{
  int<lower = 1> M; // kinds of bacterias
  int<lower = 1> N; // Total bacterias
  int<lower = 1> T; // number of obs time
  int<lower = 1> N_case; // number of cases
  int<lower = 1> N_control; // number of control

  real time[T];
  int<lower = 0, upper = N> cases[N_case, T, M];      // count of bacterias in cases
  int<lower = 0, upper = N> control[N_control, T, M]; // count of bacterias in control

}
transformed data {
  vector[T] mu;
  cov_matrix[T] Sigma;
  for (i in 1:T)
    mu[i] <- 0;
  for (i in 1:T)
    for(j in 1:T)
      Sigma[i,j] <- exp(-pow(time[i] - time[j], 2)) +
                    if_else(i == j, 0.1, 0.0);
}
parameters {
  vector[T] alpha[M];
  simplex[M] theta_case[T];
  simplex[M] theta_control[T];
}
transformed parameters{
  vector<lower=0>[M] alpha_1[T];
  for(i in 1:T)
    for(j in 1:M)
      alpha_1[i,j] <- abs( alpha[j,i] );
}
model {
  for(i in 1:M){
    alpha[i] ~ multi_normal(mu, Sigma);
  }

  for(i in 1:T){
    theta_case[i] ~ dirichlet( alpha_1[i] );
    theta_control[i] ~ dirichlet( alpha_1[i] );
  }

  for(i in 1:N_case)
   for(j in 1:T)
        cases[i,j] ~ multinomial(theta_case[j]);

  for(i in 1:N_control)
    for(j in 1:T)
        control[i,j] ~ multinomial(theta_control[j]);
}

|

